---
// Niche statement (messaging reframe, design 2026-10-20): the positioning block.
// Names and links CodexBar respectfully, no numbers, frames the smaller scope as
// a deliberate choice. Keep it free of digits and dashes (page.test.ts checks).
import { CODEXBAR } from '~/navigation';
---

<section data-niche class="not-prose mx-auto max-w-6xl scroll-mt-[72px] px-4 pt-24 sm:px-6">
  <p class="text-center text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-eyebrow">Why it stays small</p>
  <h2 class="mt-2 text-center font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-heading md:text-4xl">
    Focused on purpose.
  </h2>
  <div class="mx-auto mt-6 max-w-2xl space-y-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted">
    <p>
      AgentTacho keeps the usage and budget of every account on one screen, so you can see what is left and decide what
      to run next. It does one job and tries to do it well.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is not built to do everything. If you want the widest coverage and the longest feature list, <a
        class="font-semibold text-link underline underline-offset-4"
        href={CODEXBAR}
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">CodexBar</a
      > is a larger, more feature-rich project with many more providers, and a great choice. AgentTacho stays small on purpose,
      a quiet view of usage and budgets for the agents you actually run. You pick the tool that fits.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
